.TH "EPOCH" 1 "2026-09-30" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
epoch \- convert Unix timestamps and dates both ways
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
epoch [options] [timestamp | date]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A Unix timestamp counts the seconds since 1970\-01\-01 00:00:00 UTC. Logs, databases, JWTs, Kubernetes objects and most APIs store times this way, and none of us can read \fB1790000000\fR at a glance.
.PP
Give \fBepoch\fR a number and it prints that moment in your local time, in UTC, and how long ago it was:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
local  Mon 2026-09-21 19:43:20 IST
utc    Mon 2026-09-21 14:13:20 UTC
ago    8 days
.fi
.RE
.PP
Give it a date and it prints the timestamp:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch "2026-09-29 14:30"
1790672400
.fi
.RE
.PP
Give it nothing and it prints the timestamp of this moment.
.SS How it tells a number from a date
.PP
Input that is only digits, with an optional minus sign and an optional fraction, is a timestamp. \fB@1790000000\fR, the form \fBdate \-d\fR uses, is a timestamp too. Anything else is a date.
.PP
Many systems count in milliseconds or smaller units, and the number of digits gives it away. A timestamp for a date between 2001 and 2286 has 10 digits in seconds, 13 in milliseconds, 16 in microseconds and 19 in nanoseconds. \fBepoch\fR reads the number by its length:
.TP
up to 11
Read as: seconds. Seen in: Unix tools, JWT \fBexp\fR and \fBiat\fR, most APIs
.TP
12 to 14
Read as: milliseconds. Seen in: JavaScript \fBDate.now()\fR, Java, Elasticsearch, Kafka
.TP
15 to 17
Read as: microseconds. Seen in: PostgreSQL, Python \fBtime.time_ns() // 1000\fR
.TP
18 and 19
Read as: nanoseconds. Seen in: Go, Prometheus, Loki, containerd logs
.PP
When it reads the number as anything but seconds, it says so on stderr, \fBepoch: read as milliseconds\fR, and shows the first three digits of the fraction.
.PP
A fraction, as in \fB1790000000.5\fR, is seconds with a decimal part, which Python's \fBtime.time()\fR and many JSON logs print.
.SS The "ago" line
.PP
The last line counts from now to the timestamp in the two largest units that apply: years, days, hours, minutes and seconds. A time in the future says \fBin\fR instead of \fBago\fR. A year counts as 365 days, so over many years the count drifts by a day for each leap year.
.SS Dates it reads
.PP
\fBepoch\fR hands a date to \fBdate \-d\fR, so it reads everything GNU \fBdate\fR reads:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2026\-09\-29\fR, midnight at the start of the day
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2026\-09\-29 14:30\fR and \fB2026\-09\-29 14:30:05\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2026\-09\-29T09:00:00Z\fR and \fB2026\-09\-29T14:30:00+05:30\fR, ISO 8601 with a zone
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTue, 29 Sep 2026 09:00:00 GMT\fR, the form in HTTP headers and emails
.IP \(bu 2
\fByesterday 18:00\fR, \fBnext friday\fR, \fB2 hours ago\fR
.PP
A date without a zone is local time. \fB\-u\fR reads it as UTC. A date with a zone, such as \fBZ\fR or \fB+05:30\fR, ignores \fB\-u\fR, because it already says which zone it is in.
.PP
Words after \fBepoch\fR join with spaces, so \fBepoch 2026\-09\-29 14:30\fR works without quotes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-utc\fR
Read a date without a zone as UTC, not local time.
.TP
\fB\-\-ms\fR
Print the timestamp in milliseconds, for a date or for now.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Hide the "read as" line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print how it read the input and the \fBdate\fR command it runs. For a date, also print the date back in full, so you can check it read what you meant.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH TIME ZONES
.PP
The local line uses your time zone, from \fBTZ\fR or the system setting. Set \fBTZ\fR for one command to see a timestamp in another place:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ TZ=America/New_York epoch 1790000000
local  Mon 2026-09-21 10:13:20 EDT
utc    Mon 2026-09-21 14:13:20 UTC
ago    8 days
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBtimedatectl list\-timezones\fR lists the names.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
None. It calls \fBdate\fR with fixed options.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBdate\fR from coreutils, or BusyBox \fBdate\fR as on Alpine. BusyBox reads \fB2026\-09\-29 14:30\fR but not ISO 8601, so when it refuses a date such as \fB2026\-09\-29T09:00:00.25Z\fR, \fBepoch\fR splits off the \fBT\fR, the zone and the fraction and gives it the plain \fB2026\-09\-29 09:00:00\fR. \fB\-v\fR shows that second \fBdate\fR call. The HTTP form and words such as \fByesterday\fR still need GNU \fBdate\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A timestamp from a log
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch 1790000000
local  Mon 2026-09-21 19:43:20 IST
utc    Mon 2026-09-21 14:13:20 UTC
ago    8 days
.fi
.RE
.SS Milliseconds
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch 1790000000123
epoch: read as milliseconds
local  Mon 2026-09-21 19:43:20.123 IST
utc    Mon 2026-09-21 14:13:20.123 UTC
ago    8 days
.fi
.RE
.SS When a token expires
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch 1800000000
local  Fri 2027-01-15 13:30:00 IST
utc    Fri 2027-01-15 08:00:00 UTC
in     107 days, 16 hours
.fi
.RE
.SS A date to a timestamp
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch "2026-09-29 14:30"
1790672400
.fi
.RE
.SS The same moment, typed in UTC
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch -u 2026-09-29 09:00
1790672400
.fi
.RE
.SS Checking what it read
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch -v 2026-09-29T09:00:00Z
+ date -d 2026-09-29T09:00:00Z +%s
epoch: Tue 2026-09-29 14:30:00 IST
1790672400
.fi
.RE
.SS Milliseconds for an API
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch --ms "2026-09-29 14:30:00.250"
1790672400250
.fi
.RE
.SS Something it cannot read
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ epoch next-ish
epoch: cannot read next-ish as a timestamp or a date
Try 'epoch --help' for the options.
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBepoch: cannot read next\-ish as a timestamp or a date\fR : \fBdate \-d\fR did not understand the text. Write the date as \fB2026\-09\-29 14:30\fR, or check it with \fBdate \-d 'text'\fR.
.PP
The date is off by hours : A date without a zone is local time. For a UTC time from a log, add \fB\-u\fR, or write the zone, as in \fB2026\-09\-29T09:00:00Z\fR.
.PP
A timestamp shows a date in 1970 or far in the future : The number is in a different unit than its length suggests, such as a 13\-digit value in seconds, or a value someone cut short. Divide or multiply by 1000 and try again.
.PP
A year in the thousands, such as 5138 : An 11\-digit number reads as seconds, and 99999999999 seconds is more than 3000 years. The number is probably milliseconds with its last digits cut off.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
It worked.
.TP
1
It failed, such as a timestamp too large for \fBdate\fR to show.
.TP
2
The input is neither a timestamp nor a date.
.TP
3
\fBdate\fR is missing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBjwtpeek\fR, \fBdate(1)\fR
